    "Arsenic comes from certain bedrock. It has no taste or smell, and long-term exposure raises cancer risk." : {
      "localizations" : {
        "es" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "machine_translated",
            "value" : "El arsénico proviene de ciertos tipos de roca madre. No tiene sabor ni olor, y la exposición a largo plazo aumenta el riesgo de cáncer."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "Arsenic comes from natural bedrock. In North Carolina it is most common in wells on the Carolina Slate Belt, a band of rock across the central Piedmont." : {

    },
    "Arsenic follows geology. Tracts on the Carolina Slate Belt's metasedimentary rock have about 12% of wells over the arsenic limit, roughly 5 times the state average." : {
      "localizations" : {
        "es" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "machine_translated",
            "value" : "El arsénico depende de la geología. En los sectores censales sobre la roca metasedimentaria del Cinturón de Pizarra de Carolina (Carolina Slate Belt), aproximadamente el 12 % de los pozos superan el límite de arsénico, unas 5 veces el promedio estatal."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "Arsenic has no taste, color or smell. A lab test is the only way to know." : {

    },
    "Arsenic test results by census tract. The dark band follows the Carolina Slate Belt." : {
      "localizations" : {
        "es" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
